<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    The ELF libc-start `main` naming + prototype (`option elfmain on|off`,
    default on).

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture. Both passes clear
    it and load the same stripped in-tree ELF fixture through the CLI's own
    loader. It is a position-independent x86-64 executable whose crt1 hands
    `main` to `__libc_start_main` as a PC-relative `lea rdi,[rip+0x286]`, so
    entry oracle 4 recovers 0x1405 in both passes and the ONLY difference is
    what is said about that address.

    Pass 1 (`elfmain off`) reproduces the defect: the program's own starting
    point is one more `sub_<addr>` in the inventory, declared with whatever
    reading its body alone produces - a guessed return width and one anonymous
    slot, because this `main` reads `argc` but never `argv`.

    Pass 2 (the shipped default) applies the two things the C runtime's
    contract states about that address: the name `main`, and the declaration
    `int main(int argc, char **argv, char **envp)` - all three arguments of the
    call glibc's `__libc_start_main` makes, because the prototype is applied
    locked and declaring two would assert that a `main` reading `envp` has no
    third parameter.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/stripped_dynamic_x86_64</com>
  <com>option elfmain off</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function sub_1405</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/stripped_dynamic_x86_64</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function main</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="ELFMAIN #1: off, the entry is an anonymous sub with a body-driven signature" min="1" max="1">unsigned long sub_1405\(int4 a0,unsigned long \*a1\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ELFMAIN #2: off, the argument vector has no element type, so the string it holds is an integer" min="1" max="1">sub_1357\(a1\[1\]\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ELFMAIN #3: default, the libc-start argument is named and declared" min="1" max="1">int4 main\(int4 argc,char \*\*argv,char \*\*envp\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ELFMAIN #4: default, the declared element type reaches the call site" min="1" max="1">sub_1357\(argv\[1\]\)</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
